    "title": {
      "en": "Ecosystem, food chains and the 10 percent law"
    },
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "A pond community"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A pond has plants, fish, insects and microbes living together with water and sunlight. Together they form an ecosystem."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "10 percent law",
          "3 or 4 steps",
          "biomagnification",
          "ecosystem",
          "food chain",
          "food web",
          "humans",
          "producers, consumers, decomposers"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "An ecosystem is made of biotic components (living) and abiotic components (non-living such as water, light, soil).\nProducers make food by photosynthesis. Consumers eat others: herbivores, carnivores, omnivores. Decomposers break down dead matter and recycle nutrients.\nA food chain is a series of organisms feeding on each other: grass, grasshopper, frog, snake, eagle. Each step is a trophic level.\nMany interlinked chains form a food web.\nEnergy flows in one direction. Only about 10 percent of the energy passes to the next trophic level; the rest is lost as heat.\nBecause of this loss, food chains have only 3 or 4 steps.\nBiological magnification: harmful chemicals like pesticides build up at higher trophic levels, so top consumers carry the highest amounts.\nHumans eat at several levels, so pesticides in crops, fish and milk reach us."
        }
      },
